Risk Management

Risk management throughout the period was driven by a focus on capital preservation, controlled exposure, and maintaining flexibility in an increasingly reactive market environment. Maximum drawdown was contained to approximately -4.9%, reflecting proactive reduction of exposure during weaker market conditions and avoidance of deeper capital impairment during periods of unstable price action.

Portfolio exposure was actively managed, with an average beta of approximately 0.40 relative to Bitcoin. This lower level of directional exposure during the early phase of the month limited downside participation, while allowing for increased effective exposure as market conditions improved. This dynamic adjustment of beta was a key contributor to both drawdown control and overall performance.

Volatility was managed through selective participation and position sizing, resulting in lower realised volatility during the initial phase of the period. As conditions stabilised, increased participation enabled more efficient capture of directional moves, improving overall risk-adjusted returns. This reflects a preference for engaging in higher-quality opportunities rather than maintaining constant exposure.

A significant proportion of capital was held in cash throughout the period, providing both downside protection and strategic optionality. This allowed the portfolio to avoid forced participation during uncertain conditions and deploy capital more effectively as market structure improved. Maintaining liquidity was a deliberate component of the risk framework, rather than a passive outcome.

Overall, the portfolio’s risk approach is centred on dynamic exposure management, asymmetric positioning, and preservation of capital, with the objective of limiting downside while retaining the flexibility to scale into higher-conviction opportunities.
